---
id: middleware
title: Middleware
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-is-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What is Middleware?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware allows you to customize the behavior of both server routes like GET/POST/etc (including requests to SSR your application) and server functions created with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createServerFn"},{"type":"text","value":". Middleware is composable and can even depend on other middleware to create a chain of operations that are executed hierarchically and in order."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-kinds-of-things-can-i-do-with-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What kinds of things can I do with Middleware?"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authentication"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Verify a user's identity before executing a server function."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authorization"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Check if a user has the necessary permissions to execute a server function."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logging"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Log requests, responses, and errors."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CSP"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Configure Content Security Policy and other security measures."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observability"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Collect metrics, traces, and logs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide Context"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Attach data to the request object for use in other middleware or server functions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error Handling"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Handle errors in a consistent way."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And many more! The possibilities are up to you!"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"middleware-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are two types of middleware: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"request middleware"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server function middleware"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request middleware"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is used to customize the behavior of any server request that passes through it, including server functions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server function middleware"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is used to customize the behavior of server functions specifically."}]}]}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server function middleware is a subset of request middleware that has extra functionality specifically for server functions like being able to validate input data or perform client-side logic both before and after the server function is executed."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"key-differences","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key Differences"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request Middleware"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Function Middleware"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scope"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All server requests"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server functions only"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Methods"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Input Validation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".validator()"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-side Logic"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dependencies"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can depend on request middleware"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can depend on both types"}]}]]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request middleware cannot depend on server function middleware, but server function middleware can depend on request middleware."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"core-concepts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core Concepts"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"middleware-composition","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware Composition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All middleware is composable, which means that one middleware can depend on another middleware."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nconst authMiddleware = createMiddleware()\n  .middleware([loggingMiddleware])\n  .server(() => {\n    //...\n  })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"progressing-the-middleware-chain","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Progressing the Middleware Chain"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware is next-able, which means that you must call the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"},{"type":"text","value":" function in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server"},{"type":"text","value":" method (and/or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client"},{"type":"text","value":" method if you are creating a server function middleware) to execute the next middleware in the chain. This allows you to:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Short circuit the middleware chain and return early"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass data to the next middleware"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Access the result of the next middleware"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass context to the wrapping middleware"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(async ({ next }) => {\n  const result = await next() // <-- This will execute the next middleware in the chain\n  return result\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"request-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request Middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request middleware is used to customize the behavior of any server request that passes through it, including server routes, SSR and server functions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To create a request middleware, call the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" function. You may call this function with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type"},{"type":"text","value":" property set to 'request', but this is the default value so you can omit it if you'd like."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"available-methods","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Available Methods"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request middleware has the following methods:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"middleware"},{"type":"text","value":": Add a middleware to the chain."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server"},{"type":"text","value":": Define server-side logic that the middleware will execute before any nested middleware and ultimately a server function, and also provide the result to the next middleware."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"the-server-method","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server"},{"type":"text","value":" method"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server"},{"type":"text","value":" method is used to define server-side logic that the middleware will execute before any nested middleware, and also provide the result to the next middleware. It receives the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"},{"type":"text","value":" method and other things like context and the request object:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(\n  ({ next, context, request }) => {\n    return next()\n  },\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To quickly visualize this handshake, here is a diagram:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"sequenceDiagram\n  HTTP ->> Middleware.server: Request\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: next()\n  Middleware.server ->> ServerFn: payload\n  ServerFn ->> Middleware.server: result\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: return\n  Middleware.server ->> HTTP: Response\n\n  box Server\n  participant Middleware.server\n  participant ServerFn\n  end","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-request-middleware-with-server-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using Request Middleware with Server Routes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can use request middleware with server routes in two ways:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"all-server-route-methods","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All Server Route Methods"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To have a server route use middleware for all methods, pass a middleware array to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"middleware"},{"type":"text","value":" property of the method builder object."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/foo')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [loggingMiddleware],\n    handlers: {\n      GET: () => {\n        //...\n      },\n      POST: () => {\n        //...\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"specific-server-route-methods","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Specific Server Route Methods"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can pass middleware to specific server route methods by using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHandlers"},{"type":"text","value":" utility and passing a middleware array to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"middleware"},{"type":"text","value":" property of the method object."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/foo')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: ({ createHandlers }) =>\n      createHandlers({\n        GET: {\n          middleware: [loggingMiddleware],\n          handler: () => {\n            //...\n          },\n        },\n      }),\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-function-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Function Middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server function middleware is a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"subset"}]},{"type":"text","value":" of request middleware that has extra functionality specifically for server functions like being able to validate input data or perform client-side logic both before and after the server function is executed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To create a server function middleware, call the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" function with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type"},{"type":"text","value":" property set to 'function'."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .client(() => {\n    //...\n  })\n  .server(() => {\n    //...\n  })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"available-methods-2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Available Methods"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server function middleware has the following methods:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"middleware"},{"type":"text","value":": Add a middleware to the chain."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"validator"},{"type":"text","value":": Modify the data object before it is passed to this middleware and any nested middleware and eventually the server function."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client"},{"type":"text","value":": Define client-side logic that the middleware will execute on the client before (and after) the server function calls into the server to execute the function."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server"},{"type":"text","value":": Define server-side logic that the middleware will execute on the server before (and after) the server function is executed."}]}]}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are (hopefully) using TypeScript, the order of these methods is enforced by the type system to ensure maximum inference and type safety."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"the-client-method","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client"},{"type":"text","value":" method"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client"},{"type":"text","value":" method is used to define client-side logic that the middleware will wrap the execution and result of the RPC call to the server."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next, context }) => {\n    const result = await next() // <-- This will execute the next middleware in the chain and eventually, the RPC to the server\n    return result\n  },\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"the-validator-method","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".validator"},{"type":"text","value":" method"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"validator"},{"type":"text","value":" method is used to modify the data object before it is passed to this middleware, nested middleware, and ultimately the server function. This method should receive a function that takes the data object and returns a validated (and optionally modified) data object. It's common to use a validation library like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"zod"},{"type":"text","value":" to do this."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { zodValidator } from '@tanstack/zod-adapter'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst mySchema = z.object({\n  workspaceId: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst workspaceMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .validator(zodValidator(mySchema))\n  .server(({ next, data }) => {\n    console.log('Workspace ID:', data.workspaceId)\n    return next()\n  })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-server-function-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using Server Function Middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To have a middleware wrap a specific server function, you can pass a middleware array to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"middleware"},{"type":"text","value":" property of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createServerFn"},{"type":"text","value":" function."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { loggingMiddleware } from './middleware'\n\nconst fn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([loggingMiddleware])\n  .handler(async () => {\n    //...\n  })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To quickly visualize this handshake, here is a diagram:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"sequenceDiagram\n  ServerFn (client) ->> Middleware.client: payload\n  Middleware.client ->> Middleware.client: next()\n  Middleware.client ->> Middleware.server: Request\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: next()\n  Middleware.server ->> ServerFn: payload\n  ServerFn ->> Middleware.server: result\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: return\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.client: Response\n  Middleware.client ->> Middleware.client: return\n  Middleware.client ->> ServerFn (client): result\n\n  box Client\n  participant ServerFn (client)\n  participant Middleware.client\n  end\n\n  box Server\n  participant Middleware.server\n  participant ServerFn\n  end","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"context-management","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Context Management"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"providing-context-via-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Providing Context via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"},{"type":"text","value":" function can be optionally called with an object that has a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" property with an object value. Whatever properties you pass to this "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" value will be merged into the parent "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" and provided to the next middleware."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst awesomeMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      context: {\n        isAwesome: Math.random() > 0.5,\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([awesomeMiddleware])\n  .server(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    console.log('Is awesome?', context.isAwesome)\n    return next()\n  })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"sending-client-context-to-the-server","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sending Client Context to the Server"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client context is NOT sent to the server by default since this could end up unintentionally sending large payloads to the server."}]},{"type":"text","value":" If you need to send client context to the server, you must call the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"},{"type":"text","value":" function with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendContext"},{"type":"text","value":" property and object to transmit any data to the server. Any properties passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendContext"},{"type":"text","value":" will be merged, serialized and sent to the server along with the data and will be available on the normal context object of any nested server middleware."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst requestLogger = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .client(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    return next({\n      sendContext: {\n        // Send the workspace ID to the server\n        workspaceId: context.workspaceId,\n      },\n    })\n  })\n  .server(async ({ next, data, context }) => {\n    // Woah! We have the workspace ID from the client!\n    console.log('Workspace ID:', context.workspaceId)\n    return next()\n  })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"client-sent-context-security","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Sent Context Security"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You may have noticed that in the example above while client-sent context is type-safe, it is not required to be validated at runtime. If you pass dynamic user-generated data via context, that could pose a security concern, so "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"if you are sending dynamic data from the client to the server via context, you should validate it in the server-side middleware before using it."}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shape validation is not authorization."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A parsed UUID/number is a "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"well-formed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" identifier, not an "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"authorized"}]},{"type":"text","value":" one. If the value is going to be used as a query key, filter, or path parameter — anything that selects which row(s) get read or written — you must also verify the session principal has access to it. Otherwise a logged-in user can rewrite the value in their own request and walk other tenants' data."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst requestLogger = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .client(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    return next({\n      sendContext: {\n        workspaceId: context.workspaceId,\n      },\n    })\n  })\n  .middleware([authMiddleware]) // session loaded server-side, NOT from sendContext\n  .server(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    // 1. Validate shape\n    const workspaceId = z.string().uuid().parse(context.workspaceId)\n    // 2. Validate access — does this session principal have membership?\n    const member = await db.memberships.find({\n      userId: context.session.userId,\n      workspaceId,\n    })\n    if (!member) throw new Error('Not a member of this workspace')\n    // 3. Now safe to use as a query key.\n    return next({ context: { workspaceId } })\n  })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Always derive the session itself from a server-trusted source (a cookie + DB lookup in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":"), never from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendContext"},{"type":"text","value":". Anything the client can send, the client can lie about."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"sending-server-context-to-the-client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sending Server Context to the Client"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Similar to sending client context to the server, you can also send server context to the client by calling the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"},{"type":"text","value":" function with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendContext"},{"type":"text","value":" property and object to transmit any data to the client. Any properties passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendContext"},{"type":"text","value":" will be merged, serialized and sent to the client along with the response and will be available on the normal context object of any nested client middleware. The returned object of calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client"},{"type":"text","value":" contains the context sent from server to the client and is type-safe."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"warning","title":"Warning","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The return type of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client"},{"type":"text","value":" can only be inferred from middleware known in the current middleware chain. Therefore the most accurate return type of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"},{"type":"text","value":" is in middleware at the end of the middleware chain"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst serverTimer = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      sendContext: {\n        // Send the current time to the client\n        timeFromServer: new Date(),\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\nconst requestLogger = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([serverTimer])\n  .client(async ({ next }) => {\n    const result = await next()\n    // Woah! We have the time from the server!\n    console.log('Time from the server:', result.context.timeFromServer)\n\n    return result\n  })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"global-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global middleware runs automatically for every request in your application. This is useful for functionality like authentication, logging, and monitoring that should apply to all requests."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/start.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" file is not included in the default TanStack Start template. You'll need to create this file when you want to configure global middleware or other Start-level options."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"global-request-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Request Middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To have a middleware run for "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every request handled by Start"}]},{"type":"text","value":", create a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/start.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" file and use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createStart"},{"type":"text","value":" function to return your middleware configuration:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart, createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst myGlobalMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => {\n  return {\n    requestMiddleware: [myGlobalMiddleware],\n  }\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"request"}]},{"type":"text","value":" middleware runs before "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every request, including server routes, SSR and server functions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"csrf-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CSRF Middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server functions are same-origin RPC endpoints and should be protected from cross-site requests. If your app does not define "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/start.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", TanStack Start installs its CSRF middleware automatically for server functions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you define a custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/start.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCsrfMiddleware()"},{"type":"text","value":" explicitly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart, createCsrfMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst csrfMiddleware = createCsrfMiddleware({\n  filter: (ctx) => ctx.handlerType === 'serverFn',\n})\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => ({\n  requestMiddleware: [csrfMiddleware],\n}))","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Origin"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Referer"},{"type":"text","value":" checks compare against the incoming request URL origin. If your deployment needs to allow a different public origin, configure it on the CSRF middleware with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCsrfMiddleware({ origin: 'https://app.example.com' })"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createCsrfMiddleware()"},{"type":"text","value":" validates every request handled by the middleware. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filter: (ctx) => ctx.handlerType === 'serverFn'"},{"type":"text","value":" when installing it globally for server function protection. It verifies same-origin browser request metadata with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Sec-Fetch-Site"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Origin"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Referer"},{"type":"text","value":" headers and rejects requests that cannot be proven same-origin."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also use the same middleware to protect any other route."}]},{"type":"code","value":"export const Route = createFileRoute('/api/foo')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [createCsrfMiddleware()],\n    handlers: { GET: () => {...} }\n  }\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you define "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/start.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" without the CSRF middleware, Start shows a development warning for server function requests. If you intentionally handle CSRF another way, disable the warning:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// vite.config.ts or rsbuild.config.ts\ntanstackStart({\n  serverFns: {\n    disableCsrfMiddlewareWarning: true,\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"global-server-function-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Server Function Middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To have a middleware run for "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every server function in your application"}]},{"type":"text","value":", add it to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"functionMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" array in your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/start.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" file:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { loggingMiddleware } from './middleware'\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => {\n  return {\n    functionMiddleware: [loggingMiddleware],\n  }\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"middleware-execution-order","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware Execution Order"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware is executed dependency-first, starting with global middleware, followed by server function middleware. The following example will log in this order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalMiddleware1"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalMiddleware2"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fn"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware, createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst globalMiddleware1 = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('globalMiddleware1')\n    return next()\n  },\n)\n\nconst globalMiddleware2 = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('globalMiddleware2')\n    return next()\n  },\n)\n\nconst a = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(async ({ next }) => {\n  console.log('a')\n  return next()\n})\n\nconst b = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([a])\n  .server(async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('b')\n    return next()\n  })\n\nconst c = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware()\n  .server(async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('c')\n    return next()\n  })\n\nconst d = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([b, c])\n  .server(async () => {\n    console.log('d')\n  })\n\nconst fn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([d])\n  .server(async () => {\n    console.log('fn')\n  })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"request-and-response-modification","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request and Response Modification"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reading-modifying-the-server-response","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reading/Modifying the Server Response"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware that uses the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server"},{"type":"text","value":" method executes in the same context as server functions, so you can follow the exact same "},{"type":"link","href":"./server-functions.md#server-function-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Function Context Utilities"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to read and modify anything about the request headers, status codes, etc."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"modifying-the-client-request","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Modifying the Client Request"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware that uses the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client"},{"type":"text","value":" method executes in a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completely different client-side context"}]},{"type":"text","value":" than server functions, so you can't use the same utilities to read and modify the request. However, you can still modify the request by returning additional properties when calling the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"},{"type":"text","value":" function."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"setting-custom-headers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Setting Custom Headers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can add headers to the outgoing request by passing a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headers"},{"type":"text","value":" object to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"next"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { getToken } from 'my-auth-library'\n\nconst authMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      headers: {\n        Authorization: `Bearer ${getToken()}`,\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"header-merging-across-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Header Merging Across Middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When multiple middlewares set headers, they are "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"merged together"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Later middlewares can add new headers or override headers set by earlier middlewares:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst firstMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      headers: {\n        'X-Request-ID': '12345',\n        'X-Source': 'first-middleware',\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\nconst secondMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      headers: {\n        'X-Timestamp': Date.now().toString(),\n        'X-Source': 'second-middleware', // Overrides first middleware\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\n// Final headers will include:\n// - X-Request-ID: '12345' (from first)\n// - X-Timestamp: '<timestamp>' (from second)\n// - X-Source: 'second-middleware' (second overrides first)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also set headers directly at the call site:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await myServerFn({\n  data: { name: 'John' },\n  headers: {\n    'X-Custom-Header': 'call-site-value',\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Header precedence (all headers are merged, later values override earlier):"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Earlier middleware headers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Later middleware headers (override earlier)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Call-site headers (override all middleware headers)"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"custom-fetch-implementation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Fetch Implementation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For advanced use cases, you can provide a custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation to control how server function requests are made. This is useful for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adding request interceptors or retry logic"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using a custom HTTP client"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Testing and mocking"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adding telemetry or monitoring"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Via Client Middleware:"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst customFetchMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const customFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n      console.log('Request starting:', url)\n      const start = Date.now()\n\n      const response = await fetch(url, init)\n\n      console.log('Request completed in', Date.now() - start, 'ms')\n      return response\n    }\n\n    return next({ fetch: customFetch })\n  },\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Directly at Call Site:"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst myFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n  // Add custom logic here\n  return fetch(url, init)\n}\n\nawait myServerFn({\n  data: { name: 'John' },\n  fetch: myFetch,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"fetch-override-precedence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fetch Override Precedence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When custom fetch implementations are provided at multiple levels, the following precedence applies (highest to lowest priority):"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Priority"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Description"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1 (highest)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Call site"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"serverFn({ fetch: customFetch })"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Later middleware"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Last middleware in chain that provides "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Earlier middleware"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First middleware in chain that provides "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"createStart"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createStart({ serverFns: { fetch: customFetch } })"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5 (lowest)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" function"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key principle:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The call site always wins. This allows you to override middleware behavior for specific calls when needed."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware, createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// Middleware sets a fetch that adds logging\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const loggingFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n      console.log('Middleware fetch:', url)\n      return fetch(url, init)\n    }\n    return next({ fetch: loggingFetch })\n  },\n)\n\nconst myServerFn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([loggingMiddleware])\n  .handler(async () => {\n    return { message: 'Hello' }\n  })\n\n// Uses middleware's loggingFetch\nawait myServerFn()\n\n// Override with custom fetch for this specific call\nconst testFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n  console.log('Test fetch:', url)\n  return fetch(url, init)\n}\nawait myServerFn({ fetch: testFetch }) // Uses testFetch, NOT loggingFetch","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chained Middleware Example:"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When multiple middlewares provide fetch, the last one wins:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMiddleware, createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst firstMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const firstFetch: CustomFetch = (url, init) => {\n      const headers = new Headers(init?.headers)\n      headers.set('X-From', 'first-middleware')\n      return fetch(url, { ...init, headers })\n    }\n    return next({ fetch: firstFetch })\n  },\n)\n\nconst secondMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const secondFetch: CustomFetch = (url, init) => {\n      const headers = new Headers(init?.headers)\n      headers.set('X-From', 'second-middleware')\n      return fetch(url, { ...init, headers })\n    }\n    return next({ fetch: secondFetch })\n  },\n)\n\nconst myServerFn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([firstMiddleware, secondMiddleware])\n  .handler(async () => {\n    // Request will have X-From: 'second-middleware'\n    // because secondMiddleware's fetch overrides firstMiddleware's fetch\n    return { message: 'Hello' }\n  })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Fetch via createStart:"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can set a default custom fetch for all server functions in your application by providing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"serverFns.fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createStart"},{"type":"text","value":". This is useful for adding global request interceptors, retry logic, or telemetry:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst globalFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n  console.log('Global fetch:', url)\n  // Add retry logic, telemetry, etc.\n  return fetch(url, init)\n}\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => {\n  return {\n    serverFns: {\n      fetch: globalFetch,\n    },\n  }\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This global fetch has lower priority than middleware and call-site fetch, so you can still override it for specific server functions or calls when needed."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom fetch only applies on the client side. During SSR, server functions are called directly without going through fetch."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"environment-and-performance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Environment and Performance"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"environment-tree-shaking","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Environment Tree Shaking"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware functionality is tree-shaken based on the environment for each bundle produced."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the server, nothing is tree-shaken, so all code used in middleware will be included in the server bundle."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the client, all server-specific code is removed from the client bundle. This means any code used in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server"},{"type":"text","value":" method is always removed from the client bundle. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" validation code will also be removed."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"middleware-factories","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware Factories"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Static middlewares are created once and reused across routes. A middleware factory wraps that creation in a function, allowing it to accept parameters and behave differently depending on the caller's needs. Authorization is a common use case."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authentication (Static Base Middleware) Example:"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This middleware validates the session and injects it into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" for downstream middlewares."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Attach "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" to every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createServerFn"},{"type":"text","value":" that needs auth."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Server functions are API endpoints, so protect the endpoint that reads or mutates private data. A route "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"beforeLoad"},{"type":"text","value":" guard improves route UX, but it is not the data boundary. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./authentication-server-primitives.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authentication Server Primitives"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// middleware.ts\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { auth } from './my-auth'\n\nexport const authMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(\n  async ({ next, request }) => {\n    const session = await auth.getSession({ headers: request.headers })\n\n    if (!session) {\n      throw new Error('Unauthorized')\n    }\n\n    return await next({\n      context: { session },\n    })\n  },\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authorization (Middleware Factory) Example:"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The middleware validates access based on the dynamic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"permissions"},{"type":"text","value":" parameter, composing with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context.session"},{"type":"text","value":" is already available."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// middleware.ts\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { auth } from './my-auth'\n\nexport const authMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(\n  async ({ next, request }) => {\n    // ... (implementation from authentication example above)\n  },\n)\n\ntype Permissions = Record<string, string[]>\n\nexport function authorizationMiddleware(permissions: Permissions) {\n  return createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n    .middleware([authMiddleware])\n    .server(async ({ next, context }) => {\n      const granted = await auth.hasPermission(context.session, permissions)\n\n      if (!granted) {\n        throw new Error('Forbidden')\n      }\n\n      return await next()\n    })\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Usage in a Server Function:"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Access requirements are defined per server function, without duplicating any middleware logic."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { authorizationMiddleware } from './middleware'\n\nexport const getClients = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([\n    authorizationMiddleware({\n      client: ['read'],\n    }),\n  ])\n  .handler(async ({ context }) => {\n    return { message: 'The user can read clients.' }\n  })","lang":"tsx"}],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-middleware","text":"What is Middleware?","level":2},{"id":"what-kinds-of-things-can-i-do-with-middleware","text":"What kinds of things can I do with Middleware?","level":3},{"id":"middleware-types","text":"Middleware Types","level":2},{"id":"key-differences","text":"Key Differences","level":3},{"id":"core-concepts","text":"Core Concepts","level":2},{"id":"middleware-composition","text":"Middleware Composition","level":3},{"id":"progressing-the-middleware-chain","text":"Progressing the Middleware Chain","level":3},{"id":"request-middleware","text":"Request Middleware","level":2},{"id":"available-methods","text":"Available Methods","level":3},{"id":"the-server-method","text":"The .server method","level":3},{"id":"using-request-middleware-with-server-routes","text":"Using Request Middleware with Server Routes","level":3},{"id":"all-server-route-methods","text":"All Server Route Methods","level":4},{"id":"specific-server-route-methods","text":"Specific Server Route Methods","level":4},{"id":"server-function-middleware","text":"Server Function Middleware","level":2},{"id":"available-methods-2","text":"Available Methods","level":3},{"id":"the-client-method","text":"The .client method","level":3},{"id":"the-validator-method","text":"The .validator method","level":3},{"id":"using-server-function-middleware","text":"Using Server Function Middleware","level":3},{"id":"context-management","text":"Context Management","level":2},{"id":"providing-context-via-next","text":"Providing Context via next","level":3},{"id":"sending-client-context-to-the-server","text":"Sending Client Context to the Server","level":3},{"id":"client-sent-context-security","text":"Client-Sent Context Security","level":4},{"id":"sending-server-context-to-the-client","text":"Sending Server Context to the Client","level":3},{"id":"global-middleware","text":"Global Middleware","level":2},{"id":"global-request-middleware","text":"Global Request Middleware","level":3},{"id":"csrf-middleware","text":"CSRF Middleware","level":3},{"id":"global-server-function-middleware","text":"Global Server Function Middleware","level":3},{"id":"middleware-execution-order","text":"Middleware Execution Order","level":3},{"id":"request-and-response-modification","text":"Request and Response Modification","level":2},{"id":"reading-modifying-the-server-response","text":"Reading/Modifying the Server Response","level":3},{"id":"modifying-the-client-request","text":"Modifying the Client Request","level":3},{"id":"setting-custom-headers","text":"Setting Custom Headers","level":4},{"id":"header-merging-across-middleware","text":"Header Merging Across Middleware","level":4},{"id":"custom-fetch-implementation","text":"Custom Fetch Implementation","level":4},{"id":"fetch-override-precedence","text":"Fetch Override Precedence","level":4},{"id":"environment-and-performance","text":"Environment and Performance","level":2},{"id":"environment-tree-shaking","text":"Environment Tree Shaking","level":3},{"id":"middleware-factories","text":"Middleware Factories","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## What is Middleware?\n\nMiddleware allows you to customize the behavior of both server routes like GET/POST/etc (including requests to SSR your application) and server functions created with `createServerFn`. Middleware is composable and can even depend on other middleware to create a chain of operations that are executed hierarchically and in order.\n\n### What kinds of things can I do with Middleware?\n\n- **Authentication**: Verify a user's identity before executing a server function.\n- **Authorization**: Check if a user has the necessary permissions to execute a server function.\n- **Logging**: Log requests, responses, and errors.\n- **CSP**: Configure Content Security Policy and other security measures.\n- **Observability**: Collect metrics, traces, and logs.\n- **Provide Context**: Attach data to the request object for use in other middleware or server functions.\n- **Error Handling**: Handle errors in a consistent way.\n- And many more! The possibilities are up to you!\n\n## Middleware Types\n\nThere are two types of middleware: **request middleware** and **server function middleware**.\n\n- **Request middleware** is used to customize the behavior of any server request that passes through it, including server functions.\n- **Server function middleware** is used to customize the behavior of server functions specifically.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Server function middleware is a subset of request middleware that has extra functionality specifically for server functions like being able to validate input data or perform client-side logic both before and after the server function is executed.\n\n### Key Differences\n\n| Feature           | Request Middleware               | Server Function Middleware |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Scope             | All server requests              | Server functions only      |\n| Methods           | `.server()`                      | `.client()`, `.server()`   |\n| Input Validation  | No                               | Yes (`.validator()`)       |\n| Client-side Logic | No                               | Yes                        |\n| Dependencies      | Can depend on request middleware | Can depend on both types   |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Request middleware cannot depend on server function middleware, but server function middleware can depend on request middleware.\n\n## Core Concepts\n\n### Middleware Composition\n\nAll middleware is composable, which means that one middleware can depend on another middleware.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nconst authMiddleware = createMiddleware()\n  .middleware([loggingMiddleware])\n  .server(() => {\n    //...\n  })\n```\n\n### Progressing the Middleware Chain\n\nMiddleware is next-able, which means that you must call the `next` function in the `.server` method (and/or `.client` method if you are creating a server function middleware) to execute the next middleware in the chain. This allows you to:\n\n- Short circuit the middleware chain and return early\n- Pass data to the next middleware\n- Access the result of the next middleware\n- Pass context to the wrapping middleware\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(async ({ next }) => {\n  const result = await next() // <-- This will execute the next middleware in the chain\n  return result\n})\n```\n\n## Request Middleware\n\nRequest middleware is used to customize the behavior of any server request that passes through it, including server routes, SSR and server functions.\n\nTo create a request middleware, call the `createMiddleware` function. You may call this function with the `type` property set to 'request', but this is the default value so you can omit it if you'd like.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n```\n\n### Available Methods\n\nRequest middleware has the following methods:\n\n- `middleware`: Add a middleware to the chain.\n- `server`: Define server-side logic that the middleware will execute before any nested middleware and ultimately a server function, and also provide the result to the next middleware.\n\n### The `.server` method\n\nThe `.server` method is used to define server-side logic that the middleware will execute before any nested middleware, and also provide the result to the next middleware. It receives the `next` method and other things like context and the request object:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(\n  ({ next, context, request }) => {\n    return next()\n  },\n)\n```\n\nTo quickly visualize this handshake, here is a diagram:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n  HTTP ->> Middleware.server: Request\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: next()\n  Middleware.server ->> ServerFn: payload\n  ServerFn ->> Middleware.server: result\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: return\n  Middleware.server ->> HTTP: Response\n\n  box Server\n  participant Middleware.server\n  participant ServerFn\n  end\n```\n\n### Using Request Middleware with Server Routes\n\nYou can use request middleware with server routes in two ways:\n\n#### All Server Route Methods\n\nTo have a server route use middleware for all methods, pass a middleware array to the `middleware` property of the method builder object.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/foo')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [loggingMiddleware],\n    handlers: {\n      GET: () => {\n        //...\n      },\n      POST: () => {\n        //...\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n#### Specific Server Route Methods\n\nYou can pass middleware to specific server route methods by using the `createHandlers` utility and passing a middleware array to the `middleware` property of the method object.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/foo')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: ({ createHandlers }) =>\n      createHandlers({\n        GET: {\n          middleware: [loggingMiddleware],\n          handler: () => {\n            //...\n          },\n        },\n      }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Server Function Middleware\n\nServer function middleware is a **subset** of request middleware that has extra functionality specifically for server functions like being able to validate input data or perform client-side logic both before and after the server function is executed.\n\nTo create a server function middleware, call the `createMiddleware` function with the `type` property set to 'function'.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .client(() => {\n    //...\n  })\n  .server(() => {\n    //...\n  })\n```\n\n### Available Methods\n\nServer function middleware has the following methods:\n\n- `middleware`: Add a middleware to the chain.\n- `validator`: Modify the data object before it is passed to this middleware and any nested middleware and eventually the server function.\n- `client`: Define client-side logic that the middleware will execute on the client before (and after) the server function calls into the server to execute the function.\n- `server`: Define server-side logic that the middleware will execute on the server before (and after) the server function is executed.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> If you are (hopefully) using TypeScript, the order of these methods is enforced by the type system to ensure maximum inference and type safety.\n\n### The `.client` method\n\nThe `.client` method is used to define client-side logic that the middleware will wrap the execution and result of the RPC call to the server.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next, context }) => {\n    const result = await next() // <-- This will execute the next middleware in the chain and eventually, the RPC to the server\n    return result\n  },\n)\n```\n\n### The `.validator` method\n\nThe `validator` method is used to modify the data object before it is passed to this middleware, nested middleware, and ultimately the server function. This method should receive a function that takes the data object and returns a validated (and optionally modified) data object. It's common to use a validation library like `zod` to do this.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { zodValidator } from '@tanstack/zod-adapter'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst mySchema = z.object({\n  workspaceId: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst workspaceMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .validator(zodValidator(mySchema))\n  .server(({ next, data }) => {\n    console.log('Workspace ID:', data.workspaceId)\n    return next()\n  })\n```\n\n### Using Server Function Middleware\n\nTo have a middleware wrap a specific server function, you can pass a middleware array to the `middleware` property of the `createServerFn` function.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { loggingMiddleware } from './middleware'\n\nconst fn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([loggingMiddleware])\n  .handler(async () => {\n    //...\n  })\n```\n\nTo quickly visualize this handshake, here is a diagram:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n  ServerFn (client) ->> Middleware.client: payload\n  Middleware.client ->> Middleware.client: next()\n  Middleware.client ->> Middleware.server: Request\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: next()\n  Middleware.server ->> ServerFn: payload\n  ServerFn ->> Middleware.server: result\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: return\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.client: Response\n  Middleware.client ->> Middleware.client: return\n  Middleware.client ->> ServerFn (client): result\n\n  box Client\n  participant ServerFn (client)\n  participant Middleware.client\n  end\n\n  box Server\n  participant Middleware.server\n  participant ServerFn\n  end\n```\n\n## Context Management\n\n### Providing Context via `next`\n\nThe `next` function can be optionally called with an object that has a `context` property with an object value. Whatever properties you pass to this `context` value will be merged into the parent `context` and provided to the next middleware.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst awesomeMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      context: {\n        isAwesome: Math.random() > 0.5,\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([awesomeMiddleware])\n  .server(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    console.log('Is awesome?', context.isAwesome)\n    return next()\n  })\n```\n\n### Sending Client Context to the Server\n\n**Client context is NOT sent to the server by default since this could end up unintentionally sending large payloads to the server.** If you need to send client context to the server, you must call the `next` function with a `sendContext` property and object to transmit any data to the server. Any properties passed to `sendContext` will be merged, serialized and sent to the server along with the data and will be available on the normal context object of any nested server middleware.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst requestLogger = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .client(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    return next({\n      sendContext: {\n        // Send the workspace ID to the server\n        workspaceId: context.workspaceId,\n      },\n    })\n  })\n  .server(async ({ next, data, context }) => {\n    // Woah! We have the workspace ID from the client!\n    console.log('Workspace ID:', context.workspaceId)\n    return next()\n  })\n```\n\n#### Client-Sent Context Security\n\nYou may have noticed that in the example above while client-sent context is type-safe, it is not required to be validated at runtime. If you pass dynamic user-generated data via context, that could pose a security concern, so **if you are sending dynamic data from the client to the server via context, you should validate it in the server-side middleware before using it.**\n\n> **Shape validation is not authorization.** A parsed UUID/number is a _well-formed_ identifier, not an _authorized_ one. If the value is going to be used as a query key, filter, or path parameter — anything that selects which row(s) get read or written — you must also verify the session principal has access to it. Otherwise a logged-in user can rewrite the value in their own request and walk other tenants' data.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst requestLogger = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .client(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    return next({\n      sendContext: {\n        workspaceId: context.workspaceId,\n      },\n    })\n  })\n  .middleware([authMiddleware]) // session loaded server-side, NOT from sendContext\n  .server(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    // 1. Validate shape\n    const workspaceId = z.string().uuid().parse(context.workspaceId)\n    // 2. Validate access — does this session principal have membership?\n    const member = await db.memberships.find({\n      userId: context.session.userId,\n      workspaceId,\n    })\n    if (!member) throw new Error('Not a member of this workspace')\n    // 3. Now safe to use as a query key.\n    return next({ context: { workspaceId } })\n  })\n```\n\nAlways derive the session itself from a server-trusted source (a cookie + DB lookup in `authMiddleware`), never from `sendContext`. Anything the client can send, the client can lie about.\n\n### Sending Server Context to the Client\n\nSimilar to sending client context to the server, you can also send server context to the client by calling the `next` function with a `sendContext` property and object to transmit any data to the client. Any properties passed to `sendContext` will be merged, serialized and sent to the client along with the response and will be available on the normal context object of any nested client middleware. The returned object of calling `next` in `client` contains the context sent from server to the client and is type-safe.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> The return type of `next` in `client` can only be inferred from middleware known in the current middleware chain. Therefore the most accurate return type of `next` is in middleware at the end of the middleware chain\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst serverTimer = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      sendContext: {\n        // Send the current time to the client\n        timeFromServer: new Date(),\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\nconst requestLogger = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([serverTimer])\n  .client(async ({ next }) => {\n    const result = await next()\n    // Woah! We have the time from the server!\n    console.log('Time from the server:', result.context.timeFromServer)\n\n    return result\n  })\n```\n\n## Global Middleware\n\nGlobal middleware runs automatically for every request in your application. This is useful for functionality like authentication, logging, and monitoring that should apply to all requests.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The `src/start.ts` file is not included in the default TanStack Start template. You'll need to create this file when you want to configure global middleware or other Start-level options.\n\n### Global Request Middleware\n\nTo have a middleware run for **every request handled by Start**, create a `src/start.ts` file and use the `createStart` function to return your middleware configuration:\n\n```tsx\n// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart, createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst myGlobalMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => {\n  return {\n    requestMiddleware: [myGlobalMiddleware],\n  }\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Global **request** middleware runs before **every request, including server routes, SSR and server functions**.\n\n### CSRF Middleware\n\nServer functions are same-origin RPC endpoints and should be protected from cross-site requests. If your app does not define `src/start.ts`, TanStack Start installs its CSRF middleware automatically for server functions.\n\nIf you define a custom `src/start.ts`, add `createCsrfMiddleware()` explicitly:\n\n```tsx\n// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart, createCsrfMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst csrfMiddleware = createCsrfMiddleware({\n  filter: (ctx) => ctx.handlerType === 'serverFn',\n})\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => ({\n  requestMiddleware: [csrfMiddleware],\n}))\n```\n\nBy default, `Origin` and `Referer` checks compare against the incoming request URL origin. If your deployment needs to allow a different public origin, configure it on the CSRF middleware with `createCsrfMiddleware({ origin: 'https://app.example.com' })`.\n\nBy default, `createCsrfMiddleware()` validates every request handled by the middleware. Use `filter: (ctx) => ctx.handlerType === 'serverFn'` when installing it globally for server function protection. It verifies same-origin browser request metadata with `Sec-Fetch-Site`, `Origin`, or `Referer` headers and rejects requests that cannot be proven same-origin.\n\nYou can also use the same middleware to protect any other route.\n\n```tsx\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/foo')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [createCsrfMiddleware()],\n    handlers: { GET: () => {...} }\n  }\n})\n```\n\nIf you define `src/start.ts` without the CSRF middleware, Start shows a development warning for server function requests. If you intentionally handle CSRF another way, disable the warning:\n\n```tsx\n// vite.config.ts or rsbuild.config.ts\ntanstackStart({\n  serverFns: {\n    disableCsrfMiddlewareWarning: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Global Server Function Middleware\n\nTo have a middleware run for **every server function in your application**, add it to the `functionMiddleware` array in your `src/start.ts` file:\n\n```tsx\n// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { loggingMiddleware } from './middleware'\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => {\n  return {\n    functionMiddleware: [loggingMiddleware],\n  }\n})\n```\n\n### Middleware Execution Order\n\nMiddleware is executed dependency-first, starting with global middleware, followed by server function middleware. The following example will log in this order:\n\n- `globalMiddleware1`\n- `globalMiddleware2`\n- `a`\n- `b`\n- `c`\n- `d`\n- `fn`\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware, createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst globalMiddleware1 = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('globalMiddleware1')\n    return next()\n  },\n)\n\nconst globalMiddleware2 = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('globalMiddleware2')\n    return next()\n  },\n)\n\nconst a = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(async ({ next }) => {\n  console.log('a')\n  return next()\n})\n\nconst b = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([a])\n  .server(async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('b')\n    return next()\n  })\n\nconst c = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware()\n  .server(async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('c')\n    return next()\n  })\n\nconst d = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([b, c])\n  .server(async () => {\n    console.log('d')\n  })\n\nconst fn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([d])\n  .server(async () => {\n    console.log('fn')\n  })\n```\n\n## Request and Response Modification\n\n### Reading/Modifying the Server Response\n\nMiddleware that uses the `server` method executes in the same context as server functions, so you can follow the exact same [Server Function Context Utilities](./server-functions.md#server-function-context) to read and modify anything about the request headers, status codes, etc.\n\n### Modifying the Client Request\n\nMiddleware that uses the `client` method executes in a **completely different client-side context** than server functions, so you can't use the same utilities to read and modify the request. However, you can still modify the request by returning additional properties when calling the `next` function.\n\n#### Setting Custom Headers\n\nYou can add headers to the outgoing request by passing a `headers` object to `next`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { getToken } from 'my-auth-library'\n\nconst authMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      headers: {\n        Authorization: `Bearer ${getToken()}`,\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n```\n\n#### Header Merging Across Middleware\n\nWhen multiple middlewares set headers, they are **merged together**. Later middlewares can add new headers or override headers set by earlier middlewares:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst firstMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      headers: {\n        'X-Request-ID': '12345',\n        'X-Source': 'first-middleware',\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\nconst secondMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      headers: {\n        'X-Timestamp': Date.now().toString(),\n        'X-Source': 'second-middleware', // Overrides first middleware\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\n// Final headers will include:\n// - X-Request-ID: '12345' (from first)\n// - X-Timestamp: '<timestamp>' (from second)\n// - X-Source: 'second-middleware' (second overrides first)\n```\n\nYou can also set headers directly at the call site:\n\n```tsx\nawait myServerFn({\n  data: { name: 'John' },\n  headers: {\n    'X-Custom-Header': 'call-site-value',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n**Header precedence (all headers are merged, later values override earlier):**\n\n1. Earlier middleware headers\n2. Later middleware headers (override earlier)\n3. Call-site headers (override all middleware headers)\n\n#### Custom Fetch Implementation\n\nFor advanced use cases, you can provide a custom `fetch` implementation to control how server function requests are made. This is useful for:\n\n- Adding request interceptors or retry logic\n- Using a custom HTTP client\n- Testing and mocking\n- Adding telemetry or monitoring\n\n**Via Client Middleware:**\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst customFetchMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const customFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n      console.log('Request starting:', url)\n      const start = Date.now()\n\n      const response = await fetch(url, init)\n\n      console.log('Request completed in', Date.now() - start, 'ms')\n      return response\n    }\n\n    return next({ fetch: customFetch })\n  },\n)\n```\n\n**Directly at Call Site:**\n\n```tsx\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst myFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n  // Add custom logic here\n  return fetch(url, init)\n}\n\nawait myServerFn({\n  data: { name: 'John' },\n  fetch: myFetch,\n})\n```\n\n#### Fetch Override Precedence\n\nWhen custom fetch implementations are provided at multiple levels, the following precedence applies (highest to lowest priority):\n\n| Priority    | Source             | Description                                          |\n| ----------- | ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| 1 (highest) | Call site          | `serverFn({ fetch: customFetch })`                   |\n| 2           | Later middleware   | Last middleware in chain that provides `fetch`       |\n| 3           | Earlier middleware | First middleware in chain that provides `fetch`      |\n| 4           | createStart        | `createStart({ serverFns: { fetch: customFetch } })` |\n| 5 (lowest)  | Default            | Global `fetch` function                              |\n\n**Key principle:** The call site always wins. This allows you to override middleware behavior for specific calls when needed.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware, createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// Middleware sets a fetch that adds logging\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const loggingFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n      console.log('Middleware fetch:', url)\n      return fetch(url, init)\n    }\n    return next({ fetch: loggingFetch })\n  },\n)\n\nconst myServerFn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([loggingMiddleware])\n  .handler(async () => {\n    return { message: 'Hello' }\n  })\n\n// Uses middleware's loggingFetch\nawait myServerFn()\n\n// Override with custom fetch for this specific call\nconst testFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n  console.log('Test fetch:', url)\n  return fetch(url, init)\n}\nawait myServerFn({ fetch: testFetch }) // Uses testFetch, NOT loggingFetch\n```\n\n**Chained Middleware Example:**\n\nWhen multiple middlewares provide fetch, the last one wins:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware, createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst firstMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const firstFetch: CustomFetch = (url, init) => {\n      const headers = new Headers(init?.headers)\n      headers.set('X-From', 'first-middleware')\n      return fetch(url, { ...init, headers })\n    }\n    return next({ fetch: firstFetch })\n  },\n)\n\nconst secondMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const secondFetch: CustomFetch = (url, init) => {\n      const headers = new Headers(init?.headers)\n      headers.set('X-From', 'second-middleware')\n      return fetch(url, { ...init, headers })\n    }\n    return next({ fetch: secondFetch })\n  },\n)\n\nconst myServerFn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([firstMiddleware, secondMiddleware])\n  .handler(async () => {\n    // Request will have X-From: 'second-middleware'\n    // because secondMiddleware's fetch overrides firstMiddleware's fetch\n    return { message: 'Hello' }\n  })\n```\n\n**Global Fetch via createStart:**\n\nYou can set a default custom fetch for all server functions in your application by providing `serverFns.fetch` in `createStart`. This is useful for adding global request interceptors, retry logic, or telemetry:\n\n```tsx\n// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst globalFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n  console.log('Global fetch:', url)\n  // Add retry logic, telemetry, etc.\n  return fetch(url, init)\n}\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => {\n  return {\n    serverFns: {\n      fetch: globalFetch,\n    },\n  }\n})\n```\n\nThis global fetch has lower priority than middleware and call-site fetch, so you can still override it for specific server functions or calls when needed.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Custom fetch only applies on the client side. During SSR, server functions are called directly without going through fetch.\n\n## Environment and Performance\n\n### Environment Tree Shaking\n\nMiddleware functionality is tree-shaken based on the environment for each bundle produced.\n\n- On the server, nothing is tree-shaken, so all code used in middleware will be included in the server bundle.\n- On the client, all server-specific code is removed from the client bundle. This means any code used in the `server` method is always removed from the client bundle. `data` validation code will also be removed.\n\n## Middleware Factories\n\nStatic middlewares are created once and reused across routes. A middleware factory wraps that creation in a function, allowing it to accept parameters and behave differently depending on the caller's needs. Authorization is a common use case.\n\n**Authentication (Static Base Middleware) Example:**\n\nThis middleware validates the session and injects it into `context` for downstream middlewares.\n\n> **Attach `authMiddleware` to every `createServerFn` that needs auth.** Server functions are API endpoints, so protect the endpoint that reads or mutates private data. A route `beforeLoad` guard improves route UX, but it is not the data boundary. See [Authentication Server Primitives](./authentication-server-primitives.md).\n\n```tsx\n// middleware.ts\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { auth } from './my-auth'\n\nexport const authMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(\n  async ({ next, request }) => {\n    const session = await auth.getSession({ headers: request.headers })\n\n    if (!session) {\n      throw new Error('Unauthorized')\n    }\n\n    return await next({\n      context: { session },\n    })\n  },\n)\n```\n\n**Authorization (Middleware Factory) Example:**\n\nThe middleware validates access based on the dynamic `permissions` parameter, composing with `authMiddleware` so `context.session` is already available.\n\n```tsx\n// middleware.ts\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { auth } from './my-auth'\n\nexport const authMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(\n  async ({ next, request }) => {\n    // ... (implementation from authentication example above)\n  },\n)\n\ntype Permissions = Record<string, string[]>\n\nexport function authorizationMiddleware(permissions: Permissions) {\n  return createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n    .middleware([authMiddleware])\n    .server(async ({ next, context }) => {\n      const granted = await auth.hasPermission(context.session, permissions)\n\n      if (!granted) {\n        throw new Error('Forbidden')\n      }\n\n      return await next()\n    })\n}\n```\n\n**Usage in a Server Function:**\n\nAccess requirements are defined per server function, without duplicating any middleware logic.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { authorizationMiddleware } from './middleware'\n\nexport const getClients = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([\n    authorizationMiddleware({\n      client: ['read'],\n    }),\n  ])\n  .handler(async ({ context }) => {\n    return { message: 'The user can read clients.' }\n  })\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: middleware\ntitle: Middleware\n---\n\n## What is Middleware?\n\nMiddleware allows you to customize the behavior of both server routes like GET/POST/etc (including requests to SSR your application) and server functions created with `createServerFn`. Middleware is composable and can even depend on other middleware to create a chain of operations that are executed hierarchically and in order.\n\n### What kinds of things can I do with Middleware?\n\n- **Authentication**: Verify a user's identity before executing a server function.\n- **Authorization**: Check if a user has the necessary permissions to execute a server function.\n- **Logging**: Log requests, responses, and errors.\n- **CSP**: Configure Content Security Policy and other security measures.\n- **Observability**: Collect metrics, traces, and logs.\n- **Provide Context**: Attach data to the request object for use in other middleware or server functions.\n- **Error Handling**: Handle errors in a consistent way.\n- And many more! The possibilities are up to you!\n\n## Middleware Types\n\nThere are two types of middleware: **request middleware** and **server function middleware**.\n\n- **Request middleware** is used to customize the behavior of any server request that passes through it, including server functions.\n- **Server function middleware** is used to customize the behavior of server functions specifically.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Server function middleware is a subset of request middleware that has extra functionality specifically for server functions like being able to validate input data or perform client-side logic both before and after the server function is executed.\n\n### Key Differences\n\n| Feature           | Request Middleware               | Server Function Middleware |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Scope             | All server requests              | Server functions only      |\n| Methods           | `.server()`                      | `.client()`, `.server()`   |\n| Input Validation  | No                               | Yes (`.validator()`)       |\n| Client-side Logic | No                               | Yes                        |\n| Dependencies      | Can depend on request middleware | Can depend on both types   |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Request middleware cannot depend on server function middleware, but server function middleware can depend on request middleware.\n\n## Core Concepts\n\n### Middleware Composition\n\nAll middleware is composable, which means that one middleware can depend on another middleware.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nconst authMiddleware = createMiddleware()\n  .middleware([loggingMiddleware])\n  .server(() => {\n    //...\n  })\n```\n\n### Progressing the Middleware Chain\n\nMiddleware is next-able, which means that you must call the `next` function in the `.server` method (and/or `.client` method if you are creating a server function middleware) to execute the next middleware in the chain. This allows you to:\n\n- Short circuit the middleware chain and return early\n- Pass data to the next middleware\n- Access the result of the next middleware\n- Pass context to the wrapping middleware\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(async ({ next }) => {\n  const result = await next() // <-- This will execute the next middleware in the chain\n  return result\n})\n```\n\n## Request Middleware\n\nRequest middleware is used to customize the behavior of any server request that passes through it, including server routes, SSR and server functions.\n\nTo create a request middleware, call the `createMiddleware` function. You may call this function with the `type` property set to 'request', but this is the default value so you can omit it if you'd like.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n```\n\n### Available Methods\n\nRequest middleware has the following methods:\n\n- `middleware`: Add a middleware to the chain.\n- `server`: Define server-side logic that the middleware will execute before any nested middleware and ultimately a server function, and also provide the result to the next middleware.\n\n### The `.server` method\n\nThe `.server` method is used to define server-side logic that the middleware will execute before any nested middleware, and also provide the result to the next middleware. It receives the `next` method and other things like context and the request object:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(\n  ({ next, context, request }) => {\n    return next()\n  },\n)\n```\n\nTo quickly visualize this handshake, here is a diagram:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n  HTTP ->> Middleware.server: Request\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: next()\n  Middleware.server ->> ServerFn: payload\n  ServerFn ->> Middleware.server: result\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: return\n  Middleware.server ->> HTTP: Response\n\n  box Server\n  participant Middleware.server\n  participant ServerFn\n  end\n```\n\n### Using Request Middleware with Server Routes\n\nYou can use request middleware with server routes in two ways:\n\n#### All Server Route Methods\n\nTo have a server route use middleware for all methods, pass a middleware array to the `middleware` property of the method builder object.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/foo')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [loggingMiddleware],\n    handlers: {\n      GET: () => {\n        //...\n      },\n      POST: () => {\n        //...\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n#### Specific Server Route Methods\n\nYou can pass middleware to specific server route methods by using the `createHandlers` utility and passing a middleware array to the `middleware` property of the method object.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/foo')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: ({ createHandlers }) =>\n      createHandlers({\n        GET: {\n          middleware: [loggingMiddleware],\n          handler: () => {\n            //...\n          },\n        },\n      }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Server Function Middleware\n\nServer function middleware is a **subset** of request middleware that has extra functionality specifically for server functions like being able to validate input data or perform client-side logic both before and after the server function is executed.\n\nTo create a server function middleware, call the `createMiddleware` function with the `type` property set to 'function'.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .client(() => {\n    //...\n  })\n  .server(() => {\n    //...\n  })\n```\n\n### Available Methods\n\nServer function middleware has the following methods:\n\n- `middleware`: Add a middleware to the chain.\n- `validator`: Modify the data object before it is passed to this middleware and any nested middleware and eventually the server function.\n- `client`: Define client-side logic that the middleware will execute on the client before (and after) the server function calls into the server to execute the function.\n- `server`: Define server-side logic that the middleware will execute on the server before (and after) the server function is executed.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> If you are (hopefully) using TypeScript, the order of these methods is enforced by the type system to ensure maximum inference and type safety.\n\n### The `.client` method\n\nThe `.client` method is used to define client-side logic that the middleware will wrap the execution and result of the RPC call to the server.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next, context }) => {\n    const result = await next() // <-- This will execute the next middleware in the chain and eventually, the RPC to the server\n    return result\n  },\n)\n```\n\n### The `.validator` method\n\nThe `validator` method is used to modify the data object before it is passed to this middleware, nested middleware, and ultimately the server function. This method should receive a function that takes the data object and returns a validated (and optionally modified) data object. It's common to use a validation library like `zod` to do this.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { zodValidator } from '@tanstack/zod-adapter'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst mySchema = z.object({\n  workspaceId: z.string(),\n})\n\nconst workspaceMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .validator(zodValidator(mySchema))\n  .server(({ next, data }) => {\n    console.log('Workspace ID:', data.workspaceId)\n    return next()\n  })\n```\n\n### Using Server Function Middleware\n\nTo have a middleware wrap a specific server function, you can pass a middleware array to the `middleware` property of the `createServerFn` function.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { loggingMiddleware } from './middleware'\n\nconst fn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([loggingMiddleware])\n  .handler(async () => {\n    //...\n  })\n```\n\nTo quickly visualize this handshake, here is a diagram:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n  ServerFn (client) ->> Middleware.client: payload\n  Middleware.client ->> Middleware.client: next()\n  Middleware.client ->> Middleware.server: Request\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: next()\n  Middleware.server ->> ServerFn: payload\n  ServerFn ->> Middleware.server: result\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.server: return\n  Middleware.server ->> Middleware.client: Response\n  Middleware.client ->> Middleware.client: return\n  Middleware.client ->> ServerFn (client): result\n\n  box Client\n  participant ServerFn (client)\n  participant Middleware.client\n  end\n\n  box Server\n  participant Middleware.server\n  participant ServerFn\n  end\n```\n\n## Context Management\n\n### Providing Context via `next`\n\nThe `next` function can be optionally called with an object that has a `context` property with an object value. Whatever properties you pass to this `context` value will be merged into the parent `context` and provided to the next middleware.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst awesomeMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      context: {\n        isAwesome: Math.random() > 0.5,\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([awesomeMiddleware])\n  .server(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    console.log('Is awesome?', context.isAwesome)\n    return next()\n  })\n```\n\n### Sending Client Context to the Server\n\n**Client context is NOT sent to the server by default since this could end up unintentionally sending large payloads to the server.** If you need to send client context to the server, you must call the `next` function with a `sendContext` property and object to transmit any data to the server. Any properties passed to `sendContext` will be merged, serialized and sent to the server along with the data and will be available on the normal context object of any nested server middleware.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst requestLogger = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .client(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    return next({\n      sendContext: {\n        // Send the workspace ID to the server\n        workspaceId: context.workspaceId,\n      },\n    })\n  })\n  .server(async ({ next, data, context }) => {\n    // Woah! We have the workspace ID from the client!\n    console.log('Workspace ID:', context.workspaceId)\n    return next()\n  })\n```\n\n#### Client-Sent Context Security\n\nYou may have noticed that in the example above while client-sent context is type-safe, it is not required to be validated at runtime. If you pass dynamic user-generated data via context, that could pose a security concern, so **if you are sending dynamic data from the client to the server via context, you should validate it in the server-side middleware before using it.**\n\n> **Shape validation is not authorization.** A parsed UUID/number is a _well-formed_ identifier, not an _authorized_ one. If the value is going to be used as a query key, filter, or path parameter — anything that selects which row(s) get read or written — you must also verify the session principal has access to it. Otherwise a logged-in user can rewrite the value in their own request and walk other tenants' data.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst requestLogger = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .client(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    return next({\n      sendContext: {\n        workspaceId: context.workspaceId,\n      },\n    })\n  })\n  .middleware([authMiddleware]) // session loaded server-side, NOT from sendContext\n  .server(async ({ next, context }) => {\n    // 1. Validate shape\n    const workspaceId = z.string().uuid().parse(context.workspaceId)\n    // 2. Validate access — does this session principal have membership?\n    const member = await db.memberships.find({\n      userId: context.session.userId,\n      workspaceId,\n    })\n    if (!member) throw new Error('Not a member of this workspace')\n    // 3. Now safe to use as a query key.\n    return next({ context: { workspaceId } })\n  })\n```\n\nAlways derive the session itself from a server-trusted source (a cookie + DB lookup in `authMiddleware`), never from `sendContext`. Anything the client can send, the client can lie about.\n\n### Sending Server Context to the Client\n\nSimilar to sending client context to the server, you can also send server context to the client by calling the `next` function with a `sendContext` property and object to transmit any data to the client. Any properties passed to `sendContext` will be merged, serialized and sent to the client along with the response and will be available on the normal context object of any nested client middleware. The returned object of calling `next` in `client` contains the context sent from server to the client and is type-safe.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> The return type of `next` in `client` can only be inferred from middleware known in the current middleware chain. Therefore the most accurate return type of `next` is in middleware at the end of the middleware chain\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst serverTimer = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      sendContext: {\n        // Send the current time to the client\n        timeFromServer: new Date(),\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\nconst requestLogger = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([serverTimer])\n  .client(async ({ next }) => {\n    const result = await next()\n    // Woah! We have the time from the server!\n    console.log('Time from the server:', result.context.timeFromServer)\n\n    return result\n  })\n```\n\n## Global Middleware\n\nGlobal middleware runs automatically for every request in your application. This is useful for functionality like authentication, logging, and monitoring that should apply to all requests.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The `src/start.ts` file is not included in the default TanStack Start template. You'll need to create this file when you want to configure global middleware or other Start-level options.\n\n### Global Request Middleware\n\nTo have a middleware run for **every request handled by Start**, create a `src/start.ts` file and use the `createStart` function to return your middleware configuration:\n\n```tsx\n// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart, createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst myGlobalMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(() => {\n  //...\n})\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => {\n  return {\n    requestMiddleware: [myGlobalMiddleware],\n  }\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Global **request** middleware runs before **every request, including server routes, SSR and server functions**.\n\n### CSRF Middleware\n\nServer functions are same-origin RPC endpoints and should be protected from cross-site requests. If your app does not define `src/start.ts`, TanStack Start installs its CSRF middleware automatically for server functions.\n\nIf you define a custom `src/start.ts`, add `createCsrfMiddleware()` explicitly:\n\n```tsx\n// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart, createCsrfMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst csrfMiddleware = createCsrfMiddleware({\n  filter: (ctx) => ctx.handlerType === 'serverFn',\n})\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => ({\n  requestMiddleware: [csrfMiddleware],\n}))\n```\n\nBy default, `Origin` and `Referer` checks compare against the incoming request URL origin. If your deployment needs to allow a different public origin, configure it on the CSRF middleware with `createCsrfMiddleware({ origin: 'https://app.example.com' })`.\n\nBy default, `createCsrfMiddleware()` validates every request handled by the middleware. Use `filter: (ctx) => ctx.handlerType === 'serverFn'` when installing it globally for server function protection. It verifies same-origin browser request metadata with `Sec-Fetch-Site`, `Origin`, or `Referer` headers and rejects requests that cannot be proven same-origin.\n\nYou can also use the same middleware to protect any other route.\n\n```tsx\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/foo')({\n  server: {\n    middleware: [createCsrfMiddleware()],\n    handlers: { GET: () => {...} }\n  }\n})\n```\n\nIf you define `src/start.ts` without the CSRF middleware, Start shows a development warning for server function requests. If you intentionally handle CSRF another way, disable the warning:\n\n```tsx\n// vite.config.ts or rsbuild.config.ts\ntanstackStart({\n  serverFns: {\n    disableCsrfMiddlewareWarning: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Global Server Function Middleware\n\nTo have a middleware run for **every server function in your application**, add it to the `functionMiddleware` array in your `src/start.ts` file:\n\n```tsx\n// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { loggingMiddleware } from './middleware'\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => {\n  return {\n    functionMiddleware: [loggingMiddleware],\n  }\n})\n```\n\n### Middleware Execution Order\n\nMiddleware is executed dependency-first, starting with global middleware, followed by server function middleware. The following example will log in this order:\n\n- `globalMiddleware1`\n- `globalMiddleware2`\n- `a`\n- `b`\n- `c`\n- `d`\n- `fn`\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware, createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst globalMiddleware1 = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('globalMiddleware1')\n    return next()\n  },\n)\n\nconst globalMiddleware2 = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('globalMiddleware2')\n    return next()\n  },\n)\n\nconst a = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).server(async ({ next }) => {\n  console.log('a')\n  return next()\n})\n\nconst b = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([a])\n  .server(async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('b')\n    return next()\n  })\n\nconst c = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware()\n  .server(async ({ next }) => {\n    console.log('c')\n    return next()\n  })\n\nconst d = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n  .middleware([b, c])\n  .server(async () => {\n    console.log('d')\n  })\n\nconst fn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([d])\n  .server(async () => {\n    console.log('fn')\n  })\n```\n\n## Request and Response Modification\n\n### Reading/Modifying the Server Response\n\nMiddleware that uses the `server` method executes in the same context as server functions, so you can follow the exact same [Server Function Context Utilities](./server-functions.md#server-function-context) to read and modify anything about the request headers, status codes, etc.\n\n### Modifying the Client Request\n\nMiddleware that uses the `client` method executes in a **completely different client-side context** than server functions, so you can't use the same utilities to read and modify the request. However, you can still modify the request by returning additional properties when calling the `next` function.\n\n#### Setting Custom Headers\n\nYou can add headers to the outgoing request by passing a `headers` object to `next`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { getToken } from 'my-auth-library'\n\nconst authMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      headers: {\n        Authorization: `Bearer ${getToken()}`,\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n```\n\n#### Header Merging Across Middleware\n\nWhen multiple middlewares set headers, they are **merged together**. Later middlewares can add new headers or override headers set by earlier middlewares:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst firstMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      headers: {\n        'X-Request-ID': '12345',\n        'X-Source': 'first-middleware',\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\nconst secondMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    return next({\n      headers: {\n        'X-Timestamp': Date.now().toString(),\n        'X-Source': 'second-middleware', // Overrides first middleware\n      },\n    })\n  },\n)\n\n// Final headers will include:\n// - X-Request-ID: '12345' (from first)\n// - X-Timestamp: '<timestamp>' (from second)\n// - X-Source: 'second-middleware' (second overrides first)\n```\n\nYou can also set headers directly at the call site:\n\n```tsx\nawait myServerFn({\n  data: { name: 'John' },\n  headers: {\n    'X-Custom-Header': 'call-site-value',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n**Header precedence (all headers are merged, later values override earlier):**\n\n1. Earlier middleware headers\n2. Later middleware headers (override earlier)\n3. Call-site headers (override all middleware headers)\n\n#### Custom Fetch Implementation\n\nFor advanced use cases, you can provide a custom `fetch` implementation to control how server function requests are made. This is useful for:\n\n- Adding request interceptors or retry logic\n- Using a custom HTTP client\n- Testing and mocking\n- Adding telemetry or monitoring\n\n**Via Client Middleware:**\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst customFetchMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const customFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n      console.log('Request starting:', url)\n      const start = Date.now()\n\n      const response = await fetch(url, init)\n\n      console.log('Request completed in', Date.now() - start, 'ms')\n      return response\n    }\n\n    return next({ fetch: customFetch })\n  },\n)\n```\n\n**Directly at Call Site:**\n\n```tsx\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst myFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n  // Add custom logic here\n  return fetch(url, init)\n}\n\nawait myServerFn({\n  data: { name: 'John' },\n  fetch: myFetch,\n})\n```\n\n#### Fetch Override Precedence\n\nWhen custom fetch implementations are provided at multiple levels, the following precedence applies (highest to lowest priority):\n\n| Priority    | Source             | Description                                          |\n| ----------- | ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| 1 (highest) | Call site          | `serverFn({ fetch: customFetch })`                   |\n| 2           | Later middleware   | Last middleware in chain that provides `fetch`       |\n| 3           | Earlier middleware | First middleware in chain that provides `fetch`      |\n| 4           | createStart        | `createStart({ serverFns: { fetch: customFetch } })` |\n| 5 (lowest)  | Default            | Global `fetch` function                              |\n\n**Key principle:** The call site always wins. This allows you to override middleware behavior for specific calls when needed.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware, createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// Middleware sets a fetch that adds logging\nconst loggingMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const loggingFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n      console.log('Middleware fetch:', url)\n      return fetch(url, init)\n    }\n    return next({ fetch: loggingFetch })\n  },\n)\n\nconst myServerFn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([loggingMiddleware])\n  .handler(async () => {\n    return { message: 'Hello' }\n  })\n\n// Uses middleware's loggingFetch\nawait myServerFn()\n\n// Override with custom fetch for this specific call\nconst testFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n  console.log('Test fetch:', url)\n  return fetch(url, init)\n}\nawait myServerFn({ fetch: testFetch }) // Uses testFetch, NOT loggingFetch\n```\n\n**Chained Middleware Example:**\n\nWhen multiple middlewares provide fetch, the last one wins:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createMiddleware, createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst firstMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const firstFetch: CustomFetch = (url, init) => {\n      const headers = new Headers(init?.headers)\n      headers.set('X-From', 'first-middleware')\n      return fetch(url, { ...init, headers })\n    }\n    return next({ fetch: firstFetch })\n  },\n)\n\nconst secondMiddleware = createMiddleware({ type: 'function' }).client(\n  async ({ next }) => {\n    const secondFetch: CustomFetch = (url, init) => {\n      const headers = new Headers(init?.headers)\n      headers.set('X-From', 'second-middleware')\n      return fetch(url, { ...init, headers })\n    }\n    return next({ fetch: secondFetch })\n  },\n)\n\nconst myServerFn = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([firstMiddleware, secondMiddleware])\n  .handler(async () => {\n    // Request will have X-From: 'second-middleware'\n    // because secondMiddleware's fetch overrides firstMiddleware's fetch\n    return { message: 'Hello' }\n  })\n```\n\n**Global Fetch via createStart:**\n\nYou can set a default custom fetch for all server functions in your application by providing `serverFns.fetch` in `createStart`. This is useful for adding global request interceptors, retry logic, or telemetry:\n\n```tsx\n// src/start.ts\nimport { createStart } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport type { CustomFetch } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nconst globalFetch: CustomFetch = async (url, init) => {\n  console.log('Global fetch:', url)\n  // Add retry logic, telemetry, etc.\n  return fetch(url, init)\n}\n\nexport const startInstance = createStart(() => {\n  return {\n    serverFns: {\n      fetch: globalFetch,\n    },\n  }\n})\n```\n\nThis global fetch has lower priority than middleware and call-site fetch, so you can still override it for specific server functions or calls when needed.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Custom fetch only applies on the client side. During SSR, server functions are called directly without going through fetch.\n\n## Environment and Performance\n\n### Environment Tree Shaking\n\nMiddleware functionality is tree-shaken based on the environment for each bundle produced.\n\n- On the server, nothing is tree-shaken, so all code used in middleware will be included in the server bundle.\n- On the client, all server-specific code is removed from the client bundle. This means any code used in the `server` method is always removed from the client bundle. `data` validation code will also be removed.\n\n## Middleware Factories\n\nStatic middlewares are created once and reused across routes. A middleware factory wraps that creation in a function, allowing it to accept parameters and behave differently depending on the caller's needs. Authorization is a common use case.\n\n**Authentication (Static Base Middleware) Example:**\n\nThis middleware validates the session and injects it into `context` for downstream middlewares.\n\n> **Attach `authMiddleware` to every `createServerFn` that needs auth.** Server functions are API endpoints, so protect the endpoint that reads or mutates private data. A route `beforeLoad` guard improves route UX, but it is not the data boundary. See [Authentication Server Primitives](./authentication-server-primitives.md).\n\n```tsx\n// middleware.ts\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { auth } from './my-auth'\n\nexport const authMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(\n  async ({ next, request }) => {\n    const session = await auth.getSession({ headers: request.headers })\n\n    if (!session) {\n      throw new Error('Unauthorized')\n    }\n\n    return await next({\n      context: { session },\n    })\n  },\n)\n```\n\n**Authorization (Middleware Factory) Example:**\n\nThe middleware validates access based on the dynamic `permissions` parameter, composing with `authMiddleware` so `context.session` is already available.\n\n```tsx\n// middleware.ts\nimport { createMiddleware } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { auth } from './my-auth'\n\nexport const authMiddleware = createMiddleware().server(\n  async ({ next, request }) => {\n    // ... (implementation from authentication example above)\n  },\n)\n\ntype Permissions = Record<string, string[]>\n\nexport function authorizationMiddleware(permissions: Permissions) {\n  return createMiddleware({ type: 'function' })\n    .middleware([authMiddleware])\n    .server(async ({ next, context }) => {\n      const granted = await auth.hasPermission(context.session, permissions)\n\n      if (!granted) {\n        throw new Error('Forbidden')\n      }\n\n      return await next()\n    })\n}\n```\n\n**Usage in a Server Function:**\n\nAccess requirements are defined per server function, without duplicating any middleware logic.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { authorizationMiddleware } from './middleware'\n\nexport const getClients = createServerFn()\n  .middleware([\n    authorizationMiddleware({\n      client: ['read'],\n    }),\n  ])\n  .handler(async ({ context }) => {\n    return { message: 'The user can read clients.' }\n  })\n```\n"}
